---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const pageSchema = [
  {
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/about/#page',
    url: 'https://sitrep.md/about/',
    name: 'About sitrep - built by one person, over their own corpus',
    description:
      'sitrep is built by one developer who runs AI coding agents across four workspaces and dozens of projects - and measures every claim against that corpus before shipping it.',
    isPartOf: { '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/#website' },
    author: { '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/about/#person' },
  },
  {
    '@type': 'Person',
    '@id': 'https://sitrep.md/about/#person',
    name: 'Sander',
    description: 'Developer in Estonia; maker of sitrep, the AI project status dashboard.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About sitrep - one person, four workspaces"
  description="Who makes sitrep and why: a developer running AI coding agents across four workspaces and dozens of projects, building the AI project status dashboard against their own corpus - and measuring every claim before making it."
  schema={pageSchema}
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="mx-auto w-full max-w-5xl px-6">
    <header class="flex flex-col gap-4 pt-16 pb-12 sm:pt-20">
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-widest text-accent uppercase">About</p>
      <h1 class="max-w-3xl text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
        One person, four workspaces, too many projects
      </h1>
      <p class="max-w-prose text-lg text-ink-muted">
        sitrep is built by one developer. This page says who, why, and what
        that means for the product - because when an app asks to read your
        whole disk, "who made this" is a fair question.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="why">
      <h2 id="why" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Why sitrep exists</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          I'm Sander, a developer in Estonia. I work across four workspaces -
          freelance client projects alongside my own - each holding anywhere
          from a few projects to a dozen. I have been leaning on AI coding
          tools since autocomplete was the top-tier option, which means I got
          the modern problem early: Claude Code, Cursor and Codex sessions
          that leave behind plans, analyses and decision documents nobody
          asked for, in volumes nobody can hold in their head.
        </p>
        <p>
          The breaking point was familiar to anyone juggling projects:
          opening one after three weeks away and genuinely not knowing where
          it stood - what was decided, what was blocked, which plan was
          still true. The state existed. It was written down. It was just
          scattered across hundreds of markdown files, and I was the
          extraction layer. sitrep is that extraction layer as software: an
          AI project status dashboard - a state layer on top of the markdown
          your AI agents already generate.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="dogfood">
      <h2 id="dogfood" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Built against its own corpus</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          sitrep runs against my real working corpus every day: roughly
          54,900 markdown files across those four workspaces - most of it
          the machine-shaped bulk a working disk accumulates, exports and
          archives and vendored trees - of which about 730 are actual
          project documents. Every extraction heuristic is
          measured against that corpus before it ships - when the intuitive
          threshold for a staleness signal would have flagged half of
          everything, the measurement won and the threshold moved.
        </p>
        <p>
          The dogfooding goes one level deeper than most: sitrep's own
          strategy and decision documents live in the corpus it parses, so
          the product reads its own decision log. A regression in the
          extractor shows up as a moved number in sitrep's own docs before
          any user could see it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="border-t border-line py-12 sm:py-16" aria-labelledby="solo">
      <h2 id="solo" class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What solo means for you</h2>
      <div class="mt-5 flex max-w-prose flex-col gap-4 text-ink-muted">
        <p>
          No growth team, no roadmap by committee. The
          <a href="/principles/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">product contract</a>
          - eight promises and a binding list of things sitrep refuses to
          become - holds precisely because there is nobody to please except
          the people using it. When you write support, you reach the person
          who wrote the code.
        </p>
        <p>
          My work is private - client projects and my own - so there is no
          public repository trail to point at, and I won't pretend
          otherwise. That is exactly why this site is built the way it is:
          the claims are designed to be
          <a href="/security/" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-accent">verified, not believed</a>
          - cut the network and everything still works, read the sync
          protocol when it ships, bring your own storage. "Trust me" is not
          an argument, and sitrep never asks you to accept it as one.
        </p>
        <p class="text-ink">- Sander</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
